          "heading": "Lesson 6.1: Resistivity principles and apparent resistivity",
          "content": "### The physical mechanism and its measurable consequence\n\nThe measured potential difference generated by injected current is converted using an electrode geometry factor to apparent resistivity. The result is an effective measure of a sensitivity volume, not a local rock sample. Contact resistance and near-surface heterogeneity affect data quality.\n\n### Acquisition protocol and observable variables\n\nRecord electrode coordinates, current, voltage, polarity reversal, stacking error, contact resistance and reciprocal measurements.\n\n### The inverse ambiguity: alternative explanations\n\nClay conduction, saline water or metallic mineralization can each lower apparent resistivity; high values may indicate dry soils or lithology changes.\n\n### Worked calculation: synthetic classroom data\n\n**Question \u2014 Wenner apparent resistivity.** Wenner spacing 5 m, V=0.12 V, I=0.01 A; compute 2\u03c0a V/I.\n\n**Explicit computation:** `2*math.pi*5*0.12/0.01` = **376.9911 ohm m**. Express the result in the units requested; perform any conversion before substituting into the equation rather than concealing a factor of one thousand in a final sentence. A rounded answer should not imply accuracy better than the synthetic inputs. This arithmetic operation provides a physical scale, not a certified subsurface interpretation.\n\n**Why the calculation matters.** Apparent resistivity uses a geometric factor. **Boundary of validity:** Earth assumed homogeneous for direct interpretation. For an independent sensitivity check, change one stated input by ten percent, leaving others fixed. Explain how the result responds, and whether a plausible alternative hypothesis could produce the same scalar.\n\n### Practical: an observation that could disprove the preferred model\n\nChoose an array spacing sequence to distinguish a shallow clay lens from a deeper conductor, then explain the inevitable trade-off in resolution.\n\n**Data stewardship.** Document synthetic status, provenance, coordinates, units, processing version, license and intended use. Keep measurements in a separate file from interpolated or inversion-derived quantities. Draw a table containing at least two testable predictions, the needed instrument reading, its physical units, and one reason the observation might be biased.\n\n### Analysis note: how to decide whether the interpretation survives\n\n**Inspect the observation footprint.** Ask which wavelengths and parts of the Earth contribute to the instrument. Potential fields integrate over substantial volumes, seismic picks follow ray paths and electrode arrays have diffuse sensitivity; a pixel-sized interpretation usually exceeds the resolving claim. In the context of **resistivity principles and apparent resistivity**, explain what a reviewer should expect to see in raw data if the mechanism is credible, and what unexpected pattern would require a revised geological model.\n\n### Synthesis and interpretation checkpoint\n\nWrite a short technical conclusion that separates three items: (1) what the instrument directly records in the electrical, electromagnetic and ground-penetrating methods problem; (2) the material property or process inferred from it; and (3) a geological claim that would still be unsupported. The exercise is not passed merely by obtaining 376.9911 ohm m. Explain why the uncertainty in the assumptions can exceed calculator precision and identify a discriminating field observation.",
