          "heading": "Lesson 6.2: Electrical resistivity tomography and inversion",
          "content": "### The physical mechanism and its measurable consequence\n\nERT estimates a resistivity distribution from many electrode combinations using numerical forward models and regularization. Depth of investigation is gradual and sensitivity decreases, not a sharp maximum depth. Smooth and blocky inverse priors can produce different geometries from the same data.\n\n### Acquisition protocol and observable variables\n\nCompare observed and predicted apparent resistivity, inversion residual maps, reciprocal error and model smoothness. Mask poorly resolved cells.\n\n### The inverse ambiguity: alternative explanations\n\nAn appealing low-resistivity plume may be an inversion artefact, clay layer or unmodelled topography; it is not proof of contamination.\n\n### Worked calculation: synthetic classroom data\n\n**Question \u2014 Voltage reciprocal check.** Forward apparent-resistivity datum is 110, reciprocal is 100 ohm m; report symmetric percent difference.\n\n**Explicit computation:** `100*abs(110-100)/((110+100)/2)` = **9.52381 %**. Express the result in the units requested; perform any conversion before substituting into the equation rather than concealing a factor of one thousand in a final sentence. A rounded answer should not imply accuracy better than the synthetic inputs. This arithmetic operation provides a physical scale, not a certified subsurface interpretation.\n\n**Why the calculation matters.** Reciprocal difference is an empirical quality metric. **Boundary of validity:** It measures consistency, not all inversion bias. For an independent sensitivity check, change one stated input by ten percent, leaving others fixed. Explain how the result responds, and whether a plausible alternative hypothesis could produce the same scalar.\n\n### Practical: an observation that could disprove the preferred model\n\nRun both smooth and robust inversions and design a simple borehole sample that discriminates between them.\n\n**Acquisition design.** Define the physical source, sensor property, site geometry and independent calibration needed before collecting data. State which raw channels must survive processing and what a second researcher could reproduce. Draw a table containing at least two testable predictions, the needed instrument reading, its physical units, and one reason the observation might be biased.\n\n### Analysis note: how to decide whether the interpretation survives\n\n**State the forward equation and its domain.** Write the physical equation together with geometry, units and assumed constants. List a realistic departure that changes the prediction and distinguish a useful approximation from an unjustified physical claim. In the context of **electrical resistivity tomography and inversion**, explain what a reviewer should expect to see in raw data if the mechanism is credible, and what unexpected pattern would require a revised geological model.\n\n### Synthesis and interpretation checkpoint\n\nWrite a short technical conclusion that separates three items: (1) what the instrument directly records in the electrical, electromagnetic and ground-penetrating methods problem; (2) the material property or process inferred from it; and (3) a geological claim that would still be unsupported. The exercise is not passed merely by obtaining 9.52381 %. Explain why the uncertainty in the assumptions can exceed calculator precision and identify a discriminating field observation.",
