          "heading": "Lesson 6.3: Electromagnetic induction and skin depth",
          "content": "### The physical mechanism and its measurable consequence\n\nAlternating fields induce electric currents whose secondary response depends on conductivity, magnetic permeability, frequency, geometry and coupling. Simple skin-depth approximations assume a uniform half-space and become misleading in strongly layered or anisotropic media.\n\n### Acquisition protocol and observable variables\n\nRecord frequency, transmitter\u2013receiver separation, coil orientation, phase, height and calibration standard. Check nearby metallic objects.\n\n### The inverse ambiguity: alternative explanations\n\nA conducting utility can overshadow a regional aquifer signal. A shallow conductive layer may shield deeper information.\n\n### Worked calculation: synthetic classroom data\n\n**Question \u2014 Resistivity to conductivity.** For resistivity 25 ohm m, compute sigma=1/rho in S/m.\n\n**Explicit computation:** `1/25` = **0.04 S/m**. Express the result in the units requested; perform any conversion before substituting into the equation rather than concealing a factor of one thousand in a final sentence. A rounded answer should not imply accuracy better than the synthetic inputs. This arithmetic operation provides a physical scale, not a certified subsurface interpretation.\n\n**Why the calculation matters.** Reciprocal relation is for scalar bulk quantities. **Boundary of validity:** Anisotropic materials need tensor descriptions. For an independent sensitivity check, change one stated input by ten percent, leaving others fixed. Explain how the result responds, and whether a plausible alternative hypothesis could produce the same scalar.\n\n### Practical: an observation that could disprove the preferred model\n\nCompare ideal skin depth at two frequencies and decide which could, in principle, interrogate a deeper target under the assumed half-space conditions.\n\n**Competing models.** Choose two different geologic or instrumental explanations for the observation and write one numerical or spatial prediction on which they disagree. Reserve a new observation as a prospective test. Draw a table containing at least two testable predictions, the needed instrument reading, its physical units, and one reason the observation might be biased.\n\n### Analysis note: how to decide whether the interpretation survives\n\n**Decide a safe intended use.** Clarify whether the result is a numerical exercise, exploratory screening, academic interpretation or professional decision product. Educational calculations do not confer authority to make engineering, emergency or environmental determinations. In the context of **electromagnetic induction and skin depth**, explain what a reviewer should expect to see in raw data if the mechanism is credible, and what unexpected pattern would require a revised geological model.\n\n### Synthesis and interpretation checkpoint\n\nWrite a short technical conclusion that separates three items: (1) what the instrument directly records in the electrical, electromagnetic and ground-penetrating methods problem; (2) the material property or process inferred from it; and (3) a geological claim that would still be unsupported. The exercise is not passed merely by obtaining 0.04 S/m. Explain why the uncertainty in the assumptions can exceed calculator precision and identify a discriminating field observation.",
