          "heading": "Lesson 6.4: Ground-penetrating radar and dielectric velocity",
          "content": "### The physical mechanism and its measurable consequence\n\nRadar reflections arise mainly from contrasts in dielectric properties, and subsurface travel time must be converted to depth using velocity. Penetration varies dramatically with conductivity, moisture, frequency and antenna coupling; conductive clay strongly limits it.\n\n### Acquisition protocol and observable variables\n\nCollect common-offset profiles with time-zero calibration, trace spacing and antenna frequency; estimate velocity from hyperbolae or a measured reflector depth.\n\n### The inverse ambiguity: alternative explanations\n\nA hyperbola may come from a pipe, stone or diffraction rather than a continuous stratigraphic horizon. Depth can be severely wrong when velocity varies.\n\n### Worked calculation: synthetic classroom data\n\n**Question \u2014 EM skin depth.** Approximate skin depth 503 sqrt(rho/f) m, rho=100 ohm m and f=1000 Hz.\n\n**Explicit computation:** `503*math.sqrt(100/1000)` = **159.0626 m**. Express the result in the units requested; perform any conversion before substituting into the equation rather than concealing a factor of one thousand in a final sentence. A rounded answer should not imply accuracy better than the synthetic inputs. This arithmetic operation provides a physical scale, not a certified subsurface interpretation.\n\n**Why the calculation matters.** Frequency changes ideal electromagnetic penetration. **Boundary of validity:** Formula assumes nonmagnetic homogeneous half-space. For an independent sensitivity check, change one stated input by ten percent, leaving others fixed. Explain how the result responds, and whether a plausible alternative hypothesis could produce the same scalar.\n\n### Practical: an observation that could disprove the preferred model\n\nRepeat an interpreted buried target depth at two plausible velocities and report a range rather than a single exact number.\n\n**Error budget.** Separate repeatability, calibration bias, position error and model uncertainty in a table with units. Decide which uncertainty dominates the interpretation and what extra information would shrink it. Draw a table containing at least two testable predictions, the needed instrument reading, its physical units, and one reason the observation might be biased.\n\n### Analysis note: how to decide whether the interpretation survives\n\n**Track the reference frame.** Coordinates, vertical datums, event origin times, magnetic epochs and polarity conventions are parts of the measurement. Confusing reference frames can shift all stations coherently without producing conspicuous random residuals. In the context of **ground-penetrating radar and dielectric velocity**, explain what a reviewer should expect to see in raw data if the mechanism is credible, and what unexpected pattern would require a revised geological model.\n\n### Synthesis and interpretation checkpoint\n\nWrite a short technical conclusion that separates three items: (1) what the instrument directly records in the electrical, electromagnetic and ground-penetrating methods problem; (2) the material property or process inferred from it; and (3) a geological claim that would still be unsupported. The exercise is not passed merely by obtaining 159.0626 m. Explain why the uncertainty in the assumptions can exceed calculator precision and identify a discriminating field observation.",
