          "secNumber": "6.9",
          "heading": "Research clinic \u2014 resistivity is not a water-quality laboratory",
          "content": "Imagine a valley where one electrode line finds a conductive zone. One hypothesis is saline groundwater, another is clay-rich sediment, and a third is a cultural conductor such as buried metal. All can lower apparent resistivity, and an inverse model might display a blue lens regardless of which interpretation is correct. A resistivity map is not a chemical analysis of water and cannot establish contamination by itself.\n\nThe measured voltage/current ratio depends on electrode geometry; the familiar Wenner formula converts it to apparent resistivity for an ideal array. Because the sensitivity kernel occupies a broad volume, changing electrode spacing changes depth sensitivity but not in the manner of a perfectly focused probe. Inversion imposes smoothness or other constraints to produce a model whose predicted apparent resistivities resemble data. A different regularization can yield a different-looking structure with similar residuals. Check reciprocal measurements, electrode contact and topographic treatment before trusting the inverse image.\n\nElectromagnetic data provide another sensitivity, but field conductivity still depends on clay, water chemistry, connected porosity, frequency and geometry. A simple skin-depth equation is a useful scaling relation under a homogeneous nonmagnetic half-space. It is not a guaranteed exploration depth in a layered conductive valley. High-frequency ground-penetrating radar may be attenuated so rapidly in wet clay that a blank section is evidence of measurement limitation, not absence of an interface.\n\nDesign an integrated survey to distinguish the three hypotheses with minimal disturbance. One shallow borehole may reveal clay type, while fluid sampling directly tests dissolved ions. Independently map buried infrastructure and repeat measurements in a second season if moisture may change. The final interpretation should map where conductivity is inferred, where fluid salinity is actually measured, and where model resolution is inadequate. Conflating those layers creates an actionable-sounding but unsupported environmental claim.",
