          "secNumber": "6.10",
          "heading": "Unit investigation: reproducible synthetic dataset and experimental design",
          "content": "**Research aim.** The invented scenario is: A hypothetical valley aquifer is imaged by resistivity electrodes, frequency-domain induction and radar lines. Clay, salinity and moisture have overlapping responses; students must avoid declaring contamination from conductivity alone. Treat `datasets/unit-06.csv` as forty fictional rows deliberately designed to exercise quality control and model criticism. Read the file header before drawing any map. Plot the two relevant measured fields from unit 6; display the `quality_flag` column rather than deleting all flagged stations silently. The synthetic table contains no geographic coordinate reference system or real site identifiers and cannot be used to identify a place.\n\n**Part A \u2014 raw measurements and dimensions.** Read each column as a quantity, not as a decorative label. For electrical, electromagnetic and ground-penetrating methods, check whether the stated units are compatible with its equation and record which columns represent observed signals, instrument corrections, independent controls or model-derived quantities. Identify missing information such as date, site geology, true coordinate system, instrument orientation or calibration. If those omissions prevent a physical inversion, state the limitation and limit the task to transparent exploratory analysis. This is a stronger result than assigning fictitious precision.\n\n**Part B \u2014 repeat and quality flags.** Count the rows marked `review`. Compare summary statistics, plotted gradients and model parameters with all forty records against the subset that passes the flag. Document every change; flags indicate inspection priority, not a universal mandate to delete. Check whether questionable values cluster along position or signal amplitude, because exclusions can introduce bias. If the noise appears correlated, do not assume a root-sum-square independent-error formula without a justification.\n\n**Part C \u2014 model competition.** Describe two geophysical models that explain the synthetic observations in electrical, electromagnetic and ground-penetrating methods; they must differ in physical property, geometry or forcing rather than just parameter precision. For each hypothesis make an observable prediction about station response, uncertainty pattern, external control or independent method. Select one new measurement that would best discriminate them, and explain its predicted signal under both hypotheses. A new dense sample from the same short profile may add less independent information than a crossing line or sample.\n\n**Part D \u2014 numerical sensitivity.** Recompute one of the unit worked cases with the principal input increased by 10% and decreased by 10%. Check if the response is linear, inverse, logarithmic, quadratic or subject to another nonlinearity. Show a table with input, computed output, unit and physical assumption. Do not claim a sensitivity of the geological interpretation itself if only the toy forward equation was changed; the inverse solution may have a much broader family of possibilities.\n\n**Part E \u2014 out-of-sample prediction and reporting.** Split the synthetic observations by physical position, retaining a contiguous group as a blind holdout. Fit a deliberately simple forward trend to remaining rows and predict the held-out quantity without retuning. Compare predictive error with a baseline that uses a constant mean. State why geographic separation can be a stronger test than random point withholding. Report all transformations, quality flags, fitted parameters, residual plots and failure modes in a compact methods appendix.\n\n**Unit-specific evidence synthesis.** Electrical resistivity is a bulk property influenced by interconnected water, ions, temperature, pore geometry, clay surface conduction and mineral phases. Four-electrode apparent resistivity is not the resistivity at a single sampled depth. Electromagnetic surveys sense conductivity through inductive coupling, and radar travel time depends on effective electromagnetic velocity and attenuation. Different array configurations sample different sensitivity regions, so apparent contrasts require geometry-aware interpretation. Saltwater and clay can both yield high conductivity; dry rock, air voids and clean sand may give resistive signatures. Instrument coupling, buried utilities, cultural noise and inversion regularization add further uncertainty. Map the chain of evidence from sensor to interpretation; highlight an uncertainty not reducible by extra decimal places. In your conclusion, indicate which measurement you would acquire next if the synthetic experiment were replaced with licensed real-world data. Keep any real data sourced from the references separate from these forty invented rows.\n\n**Suggested report.** Submit a transparent CSV-reading script or spreadsheet calculation, one raw-data figure, a sensitivity plot, two candidate geological explanations, a withheld-test result and a one-page uncertainty ledger. Grade on conceptual correctness (25%), numerical units and reproducibility (25%), alternate hypotheses (20%), validation logic (20%) and scientific communication (10%). Never reward a map for appearing more certain than its underlying observations.",
